- How would you implement rotateRight128 without 128-bit type?
- Discuss performance when 128-bit shifts are not optimized by the compiler.

Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to reason about 128-bit values and apply bit-twiddling without branching. This requires cross-half logic and edge-case handling.

Key Concepts
- 128-bit decomposition into hi and lo
- a 64-bit value is a power of two if v & (v-1) == 0 and v != 0
- exactly one half nonzero implies overall power-of-two only if that half is itself a power of two

Code Example

```c
uint8_t isPowerOfTwo128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo) {
  // Exactly one half must be nonzero and that half must be a power of two
  int hiPow = (hi & (hi - 1)) == 0 && hi != 0;
  int loPow = (lo & (lo - 1)) == 0 && lo != 0;
  return ((hi == 0) ^ (lo == 0)) ? (hi ? hiPow : loPow) : 0;
}
```

Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize for a specific CPU architecture (e.g., using intrinsics)?
- How would you extend this to 256-bit values?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement a constant-time popcount-style operation using only masks and shifts, simulating a common bitmap diff in games or databases.

## Key Concepts
- Hamming weight via parallel reduction
- XOR to identify differences
- Mask sequences for bit counting

## Code Example
```cpp
int hammingDistance64(uint64 a, uint64 b){
  uint64 x = a ^ b;
  x = (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) + ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL) + ((x >> 4) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL);
  x = (x & 0x00ff00ff00ff00ffULL) + ((x >> 8) & 0x00ff00ff00ff00ffULL);
  x = (x & 0x0000ffff0000ffffULL) + ((x >> 16) & 0x0000ffff0000ffffULL);
  x = (x & 0x00000000ffffffffULL) + ((x >> 32) & 0x00000000ffffffffULL);
  return (int)x;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt the approach for 128-bit inputs?
- What are the trade-offs vs builtin popcount on modern CPUs?

## Why This Is Asked
Targeted to assess bit‑manipulation proficiency across multi‑word integers, with emphasis on branchless design and fixed work per input.

## Key Concepts
- XOR for difference on multi-word values
- 64-bit popcount with masks and shifts
- Branchless accumulation across halves

## Code Example
```javascript
// Sketch (C-like)
int hammingDistance128(uint128 a, uint128 b){
  uint64_t lo = (a.lo ^ b.lo);
  uint64_t hi = (a.hi ^ b.hi);
  lo = lo - ((lo >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  lo = (lo & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((lo >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  lo = (lo + (lo >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  uint64_t countLo = (lo * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
  hi = hi - ((hi >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  hi = (hi & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((hi >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  hi = (hi + (hi >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  uint64_t countHi = (hi * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
  return (int)(countLo + countHi);
}
```

